%N A401963 Period of row 2^m - 1 - n in the Laver table of size 2^m, for any m such that 2^m > n + 1.
%C A401963 The Laver table of size 2^m is the table of values p * q of the unique binary operation * on {1, ..., 2^m} such that p * 1 = p + 1 for p < 2^m, 2^m * 1 = 1, and p * (q * r) = (p * q) * (p * r) (Laver); the table of size 8 is in the Example section. Row p (p * 1, ..., p * 2^m) is a block of increasing numbers written one or more times; the length of this block, a power of 2, is the period of the row. The period of row 2^m - 1 - n is the same for all m with 2^m > n + 1 (Dougherty and Jech, Theorem 3.1' and Proposition 3.2).
%C A401963 For n = 2^k - 1 there is a formula: a(2^k - 1) = 2^k. The terms just before these are the periods of the first rows of the tables of size 2^k: a(2^k - 2) = A098820(k) for k >= 1 (these terms are 1, 2, 4, 4, 8, 8, 8, 8, 16, 16, ...). A098820 lists the periods of the first rows only; this sequence lists those of all rows (the last row of a table of size 2^m has period 2^m = a(2^m - 1)).
%C A401963 The first-row periods a(2^k - 2) never decrease, but even their unboundedness, a statement about finite tables, has been proved only by assuming a large cardinal axiom, that is, an axiom, beyond the usual axioms of set theory (ZFC), which implies that certain extremely large infinite sets exist. Precisely, the periods a(2^k - 2) are unbounded if there is a nontrivial elementary embedding of a rank V_lambda into itself (Laver, with a theorem of Steel; see the Wikipedia link "Rank-into-rank"). No proof of this unboundedness in ZFC is known, and none exists in primitive recursive arithmetic, a weak system of arithmetic (Dougherty and Jech, Theorems 4.4 and 9.1).
%C A401963 It has been proved without any large cardinal axiom that a(2^k - 2) = 16 for all k from 9 up to at least A(9, A(8, A(8, 254))) (Dougherty 1993; see Biane, Section 7.2). Here A is the Ackermann function (A143796): A(3, t) = 2^(t+3) - 3, and A(r+1, t) is obtained from 1 by t + 1 applications of x -> A(r, x).
%C A401963 Equivalent definition of a(n), by an array T with rows and columns numbered from 0: let T(0,k) = k for k >= 0. For n = 0, 1, 2, ... in turn, start at x = n and replace x by T(x,n) until x = 0 (each step decreases x), and let S(n) be the set of numbers visited, including n and 0. Let row n+1 of T, in columns 0, 1, 2, ..., consist of the elements of S(n) in increasing order, repeated periodically. Then a(n) = |S(n)|. T is the Laver table read backward: p * q = 2^m - T(2^m - p, 2^m - q) in the table of size 2^m (Dougherty 1996).
%C A401963 In the rule with T, every element of S(n) is a binary submask of n (it has binary 1's only where n has them) and a(n) is a power of 2, so a(n) divides A001316(n), the number of submasks of n. The proportion of n < 2^m with a(n) = 4 has a limit as m grows, but it is not known whether this limit is positive (Biane, Proposition 8.1 and Section 8). See the notes file in the Links section for proofs, sources and further data.
%H A401963 Alper Ferudun, <a href="/A401963/b401963.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..9999</a>
%H A401963 Philippe Biane, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.00548">Laver tables and combinatorics</a>, arXiv:1810.00548 [math.CO], 2018; Séminaire Lotharingien de Combinatoire 80 (2019), Article B80c. See Section 7.1 for a(0..255), written there as pi(1..256).
%H A401963 Randall Dougherty, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/math/9205202">Critical points in an algebra of elementary embeddings</a>, arXiv:math/9205202 [math.LO], 1992; Ann. Pure Appl. Logic 65 (1993), 211-241.
%H A401963 Randall Dougherty, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/math/9503204">Critical points in an algebra of elementary embeddings, II</a>, arXiv:math/9503204 [math.LO], 1995; in: Logic: From Foundations to Applications, Oxford Univ. Press, 1996, pp. 103-136.
%H A401963 Randall Dougherty and Thomas Jech, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/math/9209202">Finite left-distributive algebras and embedding algebras</a>, arXiv:math/9209202 [math.LO], 1992; Adv. Math. 130 (1997), 201-241.
