%F A401963 a(n) divides A001316(n).
%F A401963 a(n) = A001316(n) for n <= 12, but a(13) = 4 and A001316(13) = 8.
%F A401963 For k >= 2, the number of n with 3*2^(k-2) <= n < 2^k and a(n) = A001316(n) is A018819(k-1) (Biane, Theorem 9.2).
%F A401963 a(2^k - 1) = 2^k (Dougherty and Jech, Proposition 3.2).
%F A401963 a(n) = 2 if and only if n is a power of 2 (Dougherty 1996, Lemma 5.1 and Corollary 5.2).
%F A401963 a(2^j + 2^k - 1) = 2^(j+1) for 0 <= j < k (Biane, Proposition 6.1).
%F A401963 a(2^i + 2^j + 2^k - 1) = 2^(i+2) if i is even, and 2^(i+1) if i is odd, for 0 <= i < j < k (Biane, Proposition 6.2).
%F A401963 a(n + 2^k) = a(n) or 2*a(n) for n < 2^k (Dougherty and Jech, Proposition 3.2).
%F A401963 a(2^s*n + 2^s - 1) = 2^s*a(n) for n < 2^(2*s), if s is a power of 2 (Dougherty 1996, Lemmas 4.3 and 5.5).
%F A401963 a(2^k - 2) = A098820(k) for k >= 1.
%e A401963 The Laver table of size 8, with the period of each row:
%e A401963   p\q | 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 | period
%e A401963   ----+-----------------+-------
%e A401963    1  | 2 4 6 8 2 4 6 8 |   4
%e A401963    2  | 3 4 7 8 3 4 7 8 |   4
%e A401963    3  | 4 8 4 8 4 8 4 8 |   2
%e A401963    4  | 5 6 7 8 5 6 7 8 |   4
%e A401963    5  | 6 8 6 8 6 8 6 8 |   2
%e A401963    6  | 7 8 7 8 7 8 7 8 |   2
%e A401963    7  | 8 8 8 8 8 8 8 8 |   1
%e A401963    8  | 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 |   8
%e A401963 Rows 7, 6, ..., 1 give a(0..6) = 1, 2, 2, 4, 2, 4, 4; row 1 is the block 2, 4, 6, 8 written twice, so a(6) = A098820(3) = 4. Row 8 of the table of size 16 gives a(7) = 8.
%e A401963 The rule with T in the Comments section, for n = 0..3 (row 0 of T is 0, 1, 2, 3, ...):
%e A401963   n = 0: the iteration starts and ends at 0, so S(0) = {0}, a(0) = 1, and row 1 of T is 0, 0, 0, 0, ...;
%e A401963   n = 1: 1 -> T(1,1) = 0, so S(1) = {0, 1}, a(1) = 2, and row 2 is 0, 1, 0, 1, ...;
%e A401963   n = 2: 2 -> T(2,2) = 0, so S(2) = {0, 2}, a(2) = 2, and row 3 is 0, 2, 0, 2, ...;
%e A401963   n = 3: 3 -> T(3,3) = 2 -> T(2,3) = 1 -> T(1,3) = 0, so S(3) = {0, 1, 2, 3}, a(3) = 4, and row 4 is 0, 1, 2, 3, 0, 1, 2, 3, ...
%e A401963 Row 13 of T repeats the block 0, 4, 8, 12. So for n = 13 the iteration is 13 -> T(13,13) = 4 -> T(4,13) = 1 -> T(1,13) = 0, S(13) = {0, 1, 4, 13} and a(13) = 4, although 13 has 8 binary submasks.
%o A401963 (Python)
%o A401963 # T(0,k)=k; the walks stop at 0, so its row is not stored.
%o A401963 T = [None]
%o A401963 for n in range(100):
%o A401963     x, walk = n, [n]
%o A401963     while x:
%o A401963         x = T[x][n % len(T[x])]
%o A401963         walk.append(x)
%o A401963     print(len(walk))
%o A401963     T.append(sorted(walk))
